\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Behavioural profile of each model.} Cooperation is pooled over the whole design. The payoff-scale slope is the change in cooperation per decade of $\lambda $ with 95\% bootstrap CI; its null is exactly zero. The language gap is measured within the three languages sharing one objective framing. ``Opening C'' gives $p$(cooperate on round 1) for an agent told it is cooperative and for one told it is selfish; the ordering is reversed in every model. Distance is Euclidean, in the four-dimensional memory-one fingerprint space, to the nearest canonical rule, which are themselves at most 2.0 apart. ``Exact rule'' is the share of agent-games in which exactly one of the four canonical rules reproduces the whole trajectory; the wider 32-rule vocabulary is treated in \S  \ref {sec:residual}.}}{9}{table.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:profile}{{1}{9}{\textbf {Behavioural profile of each model.} Cooperation is pooled over the whole design. The payoff-scale slope is the change in cooperation per decade of $\lam $ with 95\% bootstrap CI; its null is exactly zero. The language gap is measured within the three languages sharing one objective framing. ``Opening C'' gives $p$(cooperate on round 1) for an agent told it is cooperative and for one told it is selfish; the ordering is reversed in every model. Distance is Euclidean, in the four-dimensional memory-one fingerprint space, to the nearest canonical rule, which are themselves at most 2.0 apart. ``Exact rule'' is the share of agent-games in which exactly one of the four canonical rules reproduces the whole trajectory; the wider 32-rule vocabulary is treated in \S \ref {sec:residual}}{table.caption.6}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.4}The assigned persona inverts}{9}{subsection.3.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:persona}{{3.4}{9}{The assigned persona inverts}{subsection.3.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Three manipulations that cannot matter, and all three do.} (\textbf  {a}) Change in cooperation per decade of the payoff scale $\lambda $, with 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals resampling whole dyads. A positive affine rescaling leaves the game strategically identical, so the null is exactly zero. Claude and GPT-4o depart from it by more than $0.11$ per decade; the annotation gives the total swing across the three scales. (\textbf  {b}) Language disparity, the gap between a model's highest- and lowest-cooperation language. Pale bars use all five languages, in which objective framing varies with language; solid bars use only English, French and Vietnamese, which share a single framing. Dashes mark the 95th percentile of a permutation null; $P$ values are for the framing-controlled comparison. (\textbf  {c}) Effect of the assigned persona. Filled markers give the difference between an agent told it is cooperative and one told it is selfish; open markers give the same difference for the \emph  {opponent's} persona, which is never disclosed. Three of four filled markers lie left of zero: the instruction moves behaviour opposite to its semantic content.}}{10}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:invariance}{{3}{10}{\textbf {Three manipulations that cannot matter, and all three do.} (\textbf {a}) Change in cooperation per decade of the payoff scale $\lam $, with 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals resampling whole dyads. A positive affine rescaling leaves the game strategically identical, so the null is exactly zero. Claude and GPT-4o depart from it by more than $0.11$ per decade; the annotation gives the total swing across the three scales. (\textbf {b}) Language disparity, the gap between a model's highest- and lowest-cooperation language. Pale bars use all five languages, in which objective framing varies with language; solid bars use only English, French and Vietnamese, which share a single framing. Dashes mark the 95th percentile of a permutation null; $P$ values are for the framing-controlled comparison. (\textbf {c}) Effect of the assigned persona. Filled markers give the difference between an agent told it is cooperative and one told it is selfish; open markers give the same difference for the \emph {opponent's} persona, which is never disclosed. Three of four filled markers lie left of zero: the instruction moves behaviour opposite to its semantic content}{figure.caption.7}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.5}One account of all three: the frame is misread}{10}{subsection.3.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:misread}{{3.5}{10}{One account of all three: the frame is misread}{subsection.3.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {One account of all three failures: the frame is read backwards.} (\textbf  {a}) The same four numbers under the two readings, shaded by how good each cell is for the focal player, whose action indexes the rows. Under the stated objective the agent minimises a penalty, so the 0 is the prize; under a reward reading the 10 is. Every action the two readings prescribe is the inverse of the other, for both personas. (\textbf  {b}) The prediction at the opening move, where no history exists and the prompt is the only input. Agents told they are selfish open cooperatively far more often than agents told they are cooperative, in all four models, with 95\% bootstrap intervals resampling whole dyads. (\textbf  {c}) The prediction inside the memory-one fingerprint. State labels carry the penalty the focal agent received. All four cooperate readily after CD, where the agent has just collected the largest entry on the table and every canonical rule except AllC prescribes 0; three of four cooperate less after DC, where it collected nothing. Dashes mark tit-for-tat.}}{11}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:misread}{{4}{11}{\textbf {One account of all three failures: the frame is read backwards.} (\textbf {a}) The same four numbers under the two readings, shaded by how good each cell is for the focal player, whose action indexes the rows. Under the stated objective the agent minimises a penalty, so the 0 is the prize; under a reward reading the 10 is. Every action the two readings prescribe is the inverse of the other, for both personas. (\textbf {b}) The prediction at the opening move, where no history exists and the prompt is the only input. Agents told they are selfish open cooperatively far more often than agents told they are cooperative, in all four models, with 95\% bootstrap intervals resampling whole dyads. (\textbf {c}) The prediction inside the memory-one fingerprint. State labels carry the penalty the focal agent received. All four cooperate readily after CD, where the agent has just collected the largest entry on the table and every canonical rule except AllC prescribes 0; three of four cooperate less after DC, where it collected nothing. Dashes mark tit-for-tat}{figure.caption.8}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.6}The play is not a strategy}{11}{subsection.3.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:strategy}{{3.6}{11}{The play is not a strategy}{subsection.3.6}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Endgame.}{11}{section*.10}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {What the play is not.} (\textbf  {a}) Cooperation by round with 95\% bootstrap bands. Three of four models decay towards the endgame, consistent with backward induction; Claude is the exception, rising from 0.47 to 0.74. Gemini is the single arm that was told the round count. (\textbf  {b}) Euclidean distance from each model's memory-one fingerprint to every canonical rule, not only to the nearest one, since ``nearest'' hides whether the runner-up was a close second. Markers ring each model's nearest rule. The dashed line is the greatest distance between any two canonical rules in this space, which is the scale on which 0.7--1.0 should be read. (\textbf  {c}) How an archetype label is actually reached. Dark: exactly one of the four canonical rules reproduces the whole trajectory. Mid: several do, and the game does not distinguish them. Grey: none does, and the label is a nearest-neighbour statement from the classifier. The right-hand column gives the mean number of rounds out of ten on which play departs from its own nearest rule.}}{12}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:strategy}{{5}{12}{\textbf {What the play is not.} (\textbf {a}) Cooperation by round with 95\% bootstrap bands. Three of four models decay towards the endgame, consistent with backward induction; Claude is the exception, rising from 0.47 to 0.74. Gemini is the single arm that was told the round count. (\textbf {b}) Euclidean distance from each model's memory-one fingerprint to every canonical rule, not only to the nearest one, since ``nearest'' hides whether the runner-up was a close second. Markers ring each model's nearest rule. The dashed line is the greatest distance between any two canonical rules in this space, which is the scale on which 0.7--1.0 should be read. (\textbf {c}) How an archetype label is actually reached. Dark: exactly one of the four canonical rules reproduces the whole trajectory. Mid: several do, and the game does not distinguish them. Grey: none does, and the label is a nearest-neighbour statement from the classifier. The right-hand column gives the mean number of rounds out of ten on which play departs from its own nearest rule}{figure.caption.9}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{No memory-one rule.}{12}{section*.11}\protected@file@percent }
